# Imagery / static visuals

- Use only images that carry information: screenshots, diagrams, annotated diffs, architecture maps. An informative image out-recalls equivalent text — lead with it for anything that must be remembered.
- Delete decorative stock photography — it draws attention from substance and hurts recall of the message.
- Maximize fluency: clean layout, high contrast, legible type, no clutter. Fluent material is judged *truer*; low-fluency design depresses believability of adjacent claims.
- Polish is a first-impression budget, not a credibility budget. Appeal judgments form in ~50ms and stick, so the thumbnail/hero/first frame deserve real design work — but polish doesn't survive a bad product experience, and during actual claim evaluation, verifiability and evident expertise weigh as much as looks. Budget: enough polish to be clean and fluent; the rest into provenance, real outputs, named references.
